                <pb facs="0557" n="545" sameAs="frus:frus1940v03/pg545"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 European War 1939/6253: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Greece</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >MacVeagh</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Athens</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1940-10-28T05:00:00+02:00">October 28, 1940—5
                            a.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received October 28—3:50 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>262. Following some more absurd Italian news agency charges of Greek armed action
                    in Albania, fully denied by the Greek Government yesterday, the Italian Minister
                    handed the Premier an ultimatum at 3 o’clock this morning to [expire?] at 6,
                    demanding permission to occupy certain strategic points. When the Premier asked
                    what points, the Minister said he did not know. This last I have from the
                    British Minister who was at once called in consultation. Indications are that
                    Greece will not comply. Athens is already blacked out.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">MacVeagh</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
